<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tre'r Ceiri</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tre'r Ceiri is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort on the eastern summit of Yr Eifl on the Llŷn peninsula in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Its stone rampart encloses the remains of about 150 stone huts, many still standing to shoulder height. It was occupied before the Roman conquest and through the Roman period, and is one of the best-preserved hillforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trer-ceiri.jpg" alt="Tre'r Ceiri" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Llywelyn2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tre%27r_Ceir_(best_preserved_hillfort_in_Europe,_built_200_BC)_from_Garn_Ganol;_April_2022_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
